stabilize the feed price and lower the environmental pressures, edible insects used for human food and feed are recommended as an alternative solution by the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO, 2013). Black soldier fly (BSF, Hermetia illucens), a saprophytic insect, is widely distributed in tropical, subtropical and warm temperate regions of the world (Kumar et al., 2018). There are four stages in the BSF growth cycle: egg, larva, pupa and adult. The larvae can reach up to 220 mg in weight when fed with organic materials (Salomone et al., 2017). Studies have shown that BSF larvae (BSFL) can convert FW (Win et al., 2018), human feces (Lalander et al., 2018), and animal manure (Li et al., 2011) into their own body mass. BSFL bioconversion could be affected by different substrates. The rearing substrate has effects on growth performance, waste reduction efficiency and chemical composition of BSFL (Lim et al., 2019; Meneguz et al., 2018). Poultry feed, dog food, FW, fruit and vegetables, abattoir waste, poultry manure and other wastes could be the substrates for BSFL bioconversion; the pre-pupae grown on these substrates were quite large in all cases (>210 mg/larva) except for those reared on poultry manure (165 mg/ larva) (Lalander et al., 2019). The BSF pre-pupae are rich in nutrients and can partly replace the animal feed for fish and poultry (Barragan-Fonseca et al., 2017). Because of their high oil content, BSF pre-pupae could also be considered a potential material for biodiesel production (Wong et al., 2019; Zheng et al., 2012). When BSFL bioconversion was compared with aerobic composting, it was found to reduce the treatment time by up to 1.6fold (Cai et al., 2018). Importantly, BSF adults are not attracted to human habitats or foods, neither are they a vector in disease transmission (Lalander et al., 2019). Therefore, bioconversion of organic waste by BSFL is potentially an attractive alternative for waste valorization in the new century and has recently attracted great attention. The focus of previous studies was the potential use of BSFL for biodiesel and animal feed (Surendra et al., 2016; Win et al., 2018), the digestion of different types of waste (Beskin et al., 2018) as well as the efficacy of BSFL bioconversion (Jiang, 2018; Li et al., 2018). However, limited research has been conducted on the environmental impact from a life cycle perspective. Smetana et al. (2016) conducted a life cycle assessment (LCA) study of insect production at the industrial level, which indicated that 2–5 times greater environmental benefits could be achieved by using insect-based protein powder and meat substitution when compared with traditional products. However, only the information on materials (e.g. wheat bran, water, barley grain, minerals) and energy consumption (e.g. electrical, heat) were provided, while the gaseous emissions were not tested. Salomone et al. (2017) and Allegretti et al. (2018) conducted LCA studies on the bioconversion of BSFL from pilot plants located in Southern Italy and Brazil, respectively. However, the emission data were adopted from house fly larvae (van Zanten et al., 2015). Mertenat et al. (2019) presented a study that assessed the global warming potential (GWP) of a BSFL treatment facility in Indonesia. Direct methane (CH4) and nitrous oxide (N2O) emissions from this facility were measured. The BSFL facility was compared with an open windrow composting facility; however, because of the non-transparent database (composition of food waste, energy structure, transformation of substances, etc.), there are still some limitations when comparing with other technologies. The life cycle impact of a systemized pilot plant of BSF insects was assessed with multi-season datasets (Smetana et al., 2019); the results demonstrated that both fertilizer and insect production were favorable in terms of environment impact when compared with many conventional organic fertilizers and animal- or plant-based proteins. Performance analysis of insect biorefinery of three food waste sources (brewery grains, potato peels, expired food) from the life cycle perspective was conducted (Ites et al., 2020); the results indicated that system performance from both the LCA and economics standpoint were highly dependent on the type of waste. Material flow analysis (MFA) is important and necessary for LCA and often forms the basis of life cycle inventory (LCI) (Allesch and Brunner,
2017; Jensen et al., 2017). MFA can give not only an insight into the material and substance transfer and distribution in a defined area, but also considers the material and substance flow from a systematic perspective. It is widely applied in many fields and disciplines (Klinglmair et al., 2016; Laner et al., 2016; Steindl-Rast and Lebell, 1997; Zhang et al., 2016). MFA can be utilized when designing a waste management system (Allesch and Brunner, 2017). MFA is an important basis for system analysis, such as LCA. However, studies seldom present and interpret the material flow in FW treatment systems in detail. Guo et al. (2019) conducted MFA of alternative biorefinery scenarios for FW management, but the technologies related to insects were not included. In the present study, we collected the gaseous emissions and solid samples from a FW-based BSFL bioconversion plant and linked these values to a systematic mass balance. Our study aimed to: (1) assess the material flow inside the BSFL bioconversion system from a systematic perspective; (2) provide a full LCI (including the detailed substance transfer coefficients) of the BSFL bioconversion system; and (3) reveal the environmental impact of a BSFL bioconversion plant.
2. Materials and methods
2.1. FW-based BSFL bioconversion plant
The study was conducted in a FW-based BSFL bioconversion plant located in Hangzhou, China, with the capacity to treat 15 tons of FW (wet weight, WW) per day. A schematic diagram of the plant is shown in Fig. 1. The plant received FW from households, restaurants, and canteens. The moisture content of raw FW was 76%. FW was crushed and mixed with rice hull powder at a ratio of 4:1 (WW), which was hereafter referred to as food waste feed (FWF). The moisture content of FWF was 62% and the carbon (C) to nitrogen (N) ratio (C/N) was 19. Rice hull powder acted as the bulking agent to improve air permeability and thus optimize the composting conditions during BSFL growth.
After two days at room temperature (around 25 °C), the mixture became pasty, which was suitable for BSFL bioconversion. The composition of materials in this study was determined and presented in Table 1. The moisture was measured on a WW basis, while other components (i.e., volatile solids, C, N, protein and lipid) were based on total solids (TS). In this plant, a total of 600 boxes each with a volume of 30 × 26 × 17 cm3 were used for BSFL bioconversion. Three of these boxes were chosen as parallels (n = 3) for controlled MFA experiment. Approximately 14,000 4-day-old larvae were placed in each box. The 14,000 4-day-old larvae were counted using the following steps:
