2.2. System boundary and sample collection
The functional unit was defined as bioconversion of 1 ton of FW. There were three paths of material flow in the process of FW
bioconversion by BSFL: emissions to the environment, bioconversion to BSF pre-pupae, and matured compost. Environmental benefits and burdens from the upstream and downstream processes related to the bioconversion as well as the products substitution ratio were also taken into consideration. However, the processes of waste collection, transport, products distribution, and the construction of the plant were excluded. To investigate the MFA of BSFL bioconversion of FW, a sampling schedule of both solid and gaseous samples was carried out. Operational data were acquired from the plant, including the overall mass balance and the consumption of electricity and additional materials.
2.2.1. Solid samples
In the 10-day bioconversion procedure, BSFL were fed FWF as shown in Fig. 2 every day. Substrates were mixed homogeneously before daily sampling, and one sample (0.2 kg) was taken from each box. All the solid samples were stored at −20 °C. Moisture, volatile solids (VS), total C, and total N were measured according to standard procedures (Bareither et al., 2010). The concentrations of lipid and protein were determined by using a Soxhlet device and the Kjeldahl method, respectively (Li et al., 2017; Naumann et al., 1997; Song et al., 2019).
2.2.2. Gaseous samples
