2.3. Mass balance and LCI
The BSFL bioconversion process was assessed using the wasteLCA model EASETECH (Environmental Assessment System for Environmental TECHnologies, v2.8.5), which is one of the most widely used LCA models around the world, especially for solid waste because it is good at dealing with heterogeneous materials. The development and function of this model is detailed in Clavreul et al. (2014). More detail can be found in previous studies(Andersen et al., 2012; Bernstad and la Cour Jansen, 2011; Hansen et al., 2006; Starostina et al., 2018; Zhao et al., 2015). Mass and substance flow analysis was performed using the mass balance model STAN (subSTance flow ANalysis, 2.6) (Cencic and Rechberger, 2008). The MFA was the basis for the LCI. The LCI involved input waste, additive materials, emissions, and energy consumption of each subprocess as well as the composition of final products; it was based on the principle of mass balance. The LCA was assessed using the results of the LCI in EASETECH. The LCI of BSFL bioconversion is shown in Tables 2 and 3. All the inventory data were collected and measured from this plant excluding the emissions of CH4 and N2O. The values for CH4 and N2O emissions during BSFL bioconversion were taken from the literature (Mertenat et al., 2019) (Table 2). We assumed that the postcomposting of residue showed similar emissions to general organic waste from household or kitchen waste (Table 3). This assumption was also used in Mertenat et al. (2019). We assumed that products could be utilized under the following conditions: the insect protein contained in BSF pre-pupae could provide 100% of the protein feed on a protein basis, while matured compost could provide 60% of the N in chemical fertilizer (empirical data from the plant manager).
2.4. Life cycle impact assessment (LCIA)
The results of LCI can be used to assess the life cycle impact (e.g., on the environmental impacts). Five impact categories: GWP, acidification, terrestrial eutrophication, marine eutrophication, and particulate
matter were chosen according to the related inventory. Characterization factors of the related inventories in the chosen impact categories were obtained from the International Reference Life Cycle Data System (ILCD) Handbook (Laurent et al., 2014).
