3. Results and discussion
3.1. MFA
One ton of FW coupled with 0.25 ton of rice hull powder yielded 31 kg of dried BSF pre-pupae and 300 kg of matured compost (WW) after BSFL bioconversion and post-composting, with the remainder emitted as exhaust gas to the environment. These outputs were consistent with Salomone et al. (2017), where 30 kg of dried larvae and 335 kg of matured compost were obtained from a similar BSF processing of 1 t of FW. The initial water, C and N contents were 64% WW, 48% TS, and 2.35% TS, respectively, whereas those in the BSFL residue were 35% WW, 41% TS and 2.04% TS, respectively, after 10-day bioconversion (Fig. 3). When treating FW by BSFL in Liu et al. (2020), N content increased sharply and was consistent with the results of our study, increasing from 2.69% TS to 3.19% TS, with 3.02% TS in the BSFL residue. Fig. 4 presents the material flows during BSFL bioconversion in this study. We assumed that rice hull powder acted only as a bulking agent in FWF and was not substantially taken up by BSFL. Focusing on the outputs from the bioconversion process, the total input of FWF
(FW + rice hull powder) (WW) was distributed into BSF pre-pupae (10%), BSF residue (40%) and gaseous emissions (50%). Because of the properties of the separator, three-quarters of pre-pupae were separated and one-quarter remained in the residue. The BSF pre-pupae drying and the residue post-composting were done after separation. Therefore, the material distribution (in WW) of the whole process (including BSFL bioconversion, pre-pupae and residue separation and residue postcomposting) was 8% to fresh pre-pupae, 24% to the matured compost and 68% to the environment as gaseous emissions (50% from the bioconversion process plus 18% from post-composting). The distribution based on dry matter was 43% to emissions, 6% to BSF pre-pupae and 51% to the matured compost. The material flow of total mass, water, C and N in the BSFL bioconversion system is shown in Fig. 4. A significant proportion of water (84% of total water) was emitted to the environment, 8% was transformed to fresh BSF pre-pupae, and 8% remained in the mature compost. C flow analysis showed that 43% of C in FWF was emitted to the environment, 52% remained in matured compost, and 5% was stored in pre-pupae. N flow analysis showed that 53% of N was emitted to the environment, 42% was kept in matured compost, and 5% was stored in pre-pupae, which is in accordance with the findings by Lalander et al. (2014). During BSFL conversion of pig manure of different moisture content, the N in BSFL of all the investigated treatments accounted for 1.03–12.67% of the initial total N (Chen et al., 2019). The results of our study demonstrated that bioconversion of FWF by BSFL could achieve a significant reduction in waste mass as well as individual components, which is in accordance with the findings in other studies. It was reported that the reduction in fresh mass was 68% for municipal organic waste (Diener et al., 2011). Rehman et al., 2017 also showed reductions of water, C and N of 73%, 52.4–71.9% and 24–56% respectively, in organic waste treated by BSFL. An average reduction of 49% in dry mass of polluted corn leaves was achieved by BSFL (Bulak et al., 2018). Reductions of 55% N and 62% C were achieved by Newton et al. (2005).
