composition of the final products. NH3 emissions from both BSFL bioconversion and residue post-composting were measured in this study. The concentrations of CH4 and N2O were lower than the detection limits (100 ppm for CH4 and 0.05 ppm for N2O) in the present study. As mentioned above, the values for CH4 and N2O emissions during BSFL bioconversion were taken from the literature (Mertenat et al., 2019) (Table 2). We assumed that the post-composting of residue showed similar emissions to general organic household waste (Table 3), as used in Mertenat et al. (2019). When 1 ton of FW was treated by BSFL, 0.26 kg of CH4, 1.72 kg of NH3 and 0.037 kg of N2O could be emitted during the whole process (includes both BSFL bioconversion and post-composting). The post-composting process after BSF pre-pupae bioconversion was the main source of gaseous emission, which was consistent with Mertenat et al. (2019). LCI showed that 51% of N contained in FW was emitted to the environment in the bioconversion process, and 2% of the emitted N was in the form of NH3. Thus, 1.02% of the initial N contained in FW was emitted as NH3 during the bioconversion procedure. When converting pig manure by BSFL, N loss in the form of NH3 accounted for about 1.47–2.36% of the initial total N and N2O emission was minimal (Chen et al., 2019). While NH3 emissions were very low in the present study, no NH3 emissions were detected by Ermolaev et al. (2019) who performed fly larvae composting in 11 cm deep small boxes with 700 larvae. During the post-composting process, 34% of N contained in the solids used for post-composting was emitted, mainly as NH3. Our study demonstrated that the emissions of NH3 from the BSFL bioconversion and the post-composting processes were 0.13 kg/t and 1.59 kg/t FW, respectively, which was consistent with the results of a previous BSFL study demonstrating NH3 emission of 0.15–1.69 kg/t from BSFL bioconversion (Pang et al., 2020). Energy consumption was measured for the whole process, including egg hatching, FW crushing, BSFL bioconversion, products and residue separation, BSF pre-pupae drying as well as the post-composting process (Table 2). The highest energy consumption occurred in the BSF pre-pupae drying process, accounting for 35% of the total energy consumption. The composition of the final products of bioconversion, i.e., BSF pre-pupae and matured compost, is shown in Table 2. BSF prepupae and matured compost can be used as substitutes for animal feed and chemical fertilizer, respectively. The BSF pre-pupae were rich in protein and lipid (41 ± 0.43%TS and 47 ± 1.51%TS, respectively). This would be acceptable as fish meal or poultry nutrition (Henry et al., 2015; Khan, 2018; Liu et al., 2017). When used as feed, the mass ratio of BSF dried pre-pupae was recommended as 10% (WW) of the weight of feed (Huailong and Xiaoyan, 2018; Shisheng, 2016). We assumed that the insect protein contained in BSF pre-pupae could substitute for 100% of the protein feed on a protein basis. The upstream process of producing protein feed was selected from Ecoinvent (Ecoinvent, 2007) with the protein content of 100% TS. The heavy metals content as well as quality were within the standards for organic fertilizer in China (NY 525-2012). Matured compost resulting from the bioconversion described here was estimated to substitute 60% of chemical fertilizer on a N basis (empirical data from the plant manager).
3.3. LCIA
The overall GWP of the BSFL bioconversion system was 17.36 kg CO2-eq/t FW (Fig. 5 (a)). The specific quantities were − 72.69 kg CO2eq/t FW for the utilization of products, 17.24 kg CO2-eq/t FW from emissions and 72.81 kg CO2-eq/t FW for energy consumption. Energy consumption and greenhouse gas emissions during the whole process were taken as loads to the environment. Hence, when they were balanced with the substitution of raw materials or products from the BSFL bioconversion, CO2 net emissions were saved. Results from
Salomone et al. (2017) showed emission from each ton of FW of 30.2 kg CO2 eq in terms of GWP. The reduction in environmental impacts when compared with conventional composting was mostly caused by replacement of the production of N fertilizers. Drying accounted for the largest proportion (35%) of the total energy consumption. Van Zanten et al. (2015) and Mertenat et al. (2019) also demonstrated that energy consumption was the main contributor to GWP impact in the system of either BSFL or housefly larvae growing on FW. Salomone et al. (2017) also showed that high GWP impacts were caused by electricity consumption during the production of feed and compost, whereas drying was associated with the most significant energy consumption in the bioconversion system. The GWP caused by emissions from the post-composting process was 5-fold higher than that from BSFL bioconversion. N2O emitted during the postcomposting process was the main contributor to the a GWP of 310 kg CO2-eq (Hayes et al., 2016). Acidification was commonly related to the pollution caused by Ncompounds. The comprehensive acidification impact was 5.54 kg SO2eq/t FW. The emissions from post-composting contributed most to the acidification impact (Fig. 5 (b)). The high NH3 emission during the postcomposting process led to a serious acidification load. The acidification related to products utilization was −0.11 kg SO2-eq/t FW, and this saving on acidification impact was mainly because of the avoidance of production of the equivalent amount of inorganic fertilizer. Nevertheless, when compared with the post-composting process, the impacts related to products substitution and energy consumption were insignificant. The total impact on terrestrial eutrophication related to NH3 emission was 24.05 mol N-eq (Fig. 5 (c)). The largest burden came from the emissions during the post-composting process, with 21.36 mol Neq mainly caused by the emission of NH3. The impact on marine eutrophication was associated with NH4+ generation and release to the environment (Peigné and Girardin, 2004). The total impact on marine eutrophication was 0.54 kg N-eq (Fig. 5 (d)). The marine eutrophication caused by products utilization was 0.32 kg N-eq, 0.16 kg N-eq in emissions and 0.064 kg N-eq in energy consumption. Analysis of the contributing factors showed that the largest burden came from the utilization of compost because of the NH4+ conversion to nitrate that was removed via leaching and run-off. The overall particulate matter for the system was 0.18 kg PM2.5-eq/t FW (Fig. 5 (e)). Particulate matter was associated with emissions and energy consumptions of 0.11 and 0.08 kg PM2.5-eq/t FW, respectively. The substitution and utilization of feed and matured compost resulted in −0.02 kg PM2.5-eq/t FW savings. NH3 emissions from postcomposting was the main contributor to particulate matter. When compared with the loadings from emissions and energy consumption, the saving by product utilization was negligible. The results on the impacts of acidification, terrestrial eutrophication, marine eutrophication, and particulate matter in this study filled an important knowledge gap in the environmental impact of the BSFL bioconversion system. Contribution analysis could help us find the “hot spots” in the chosen environmental categories. The top three processes with respect to the GWP were chemical fertilizer substitution, energy consumption of drying and emissions from post-composting. For acidification and terrestrial eutrophication, the most important component was the emissions from post-composting. In marine eutrophication, chemical fertilizer substitution played an important role followed by emissions from post-composting and protein feed substitution. Emissions from post-composting, energy consumption for drying and bioconversion were the main contributors for particulate matter. Post-composting and chemical fertilizer substitution were recognized as the key processes in contribution analysis, which is consistent with the conclusions of other's study (Salomone et al., 2017). Therefore, greater attention should be paid to the emissions from post-composting and the utilization of products. The results from the hot spots could motivate us to find better ways to deal with the BSFL residues from bioconversion.
