4. Conclusions
This study explored the environmental impacts (GWP, acidification, terrestrial and marine eutrophication, and particulate matter) of FW bioconversion by BSFL using MFA. Totals of 31 kg of dried BSF prepupae (from 92 kg of WW pre-pupae) and 300 kg of matured compost (WW) were obtained from the bioconversion of 1 ton of FW coupled with 0.25 ton of rice hull powder (WW, with 62% moisture content in the mixture) by BSFL. In terms of elements, 48% of C and 58% of N were transformed into BSFL and emitted to the environment. The process of pre-pupae drying caused the largest energy consumption, which was strongly related to the environmental impact measured by GWP. LCA showed that the GWP of BSFL bioconversion was 17.36 kg CO2-eq/t FW, 5.54 kg SO2-eq/t FW for acidification, 24.05 mol N-eq/t FW for terrestrial eutrophication, 0.54 kg N-eq NH3/t FW for marine eutrophication, and 0.18 kg PM2.5-eq/t FW for particulate matter. A detailed LCI, covering the comprehensive transfer coefficients of BSFL bioconversion, provided in this study could be used for comparisons with other similar FW disposal plants.
